\fs29\fsmilli14667 \cf0 \strokec2 Mira leaned closer, her ear pushing against the door.
\fs24 \strokec2 \

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \strokec2 It opened, and she collapsed inside.
\fs24 \strokec2 \
\

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \strokec2 "Hey! What are you doing here?"
\fs24 \strokec2 \
\

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \strokec2 Mira gasped, her eyes widening. She realised that it was over: she had been caught.
\fs24 \strokec2 \

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \strokec2 The two men restrained her before she could even get to her feet, and her arms were pinned down. She was dragged away deeper into the tank, where she had never been before. She was blindfolded and gagged, and she finally realised the power that the officials in the tank had, the power to make anyone disappear without a trace. Sara must have figured it out too, and she was probably already gone.\'a0
\fs24 \strokec2 \
\

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \strokec2 Mira was taken to a windowless room, deeper than any civilian area. She knew by the pressure in her ears that they were very deep in the tankr, and that this might be a part of the tank that no civilian even knew about. While she couldn't see, she felt that she was restrained to a chair, in a dark place. She couldn't do anything but think of Sara, and how maybe she could have prevented her death.
\fs24 \strokec2 \
\

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \strokec2 Minutes and hours passed, and Mira started to lose track of time. Was her life going to end here? Has anyone noticed her disappearance? Will the hope of escape end with her? Suddenly, the door slammed open. Three people walked in, and Mira's blindfold was carefully removed. She looked up and saw President Veyr. The guards left, leaving the door closed, and Veyr sat down across from Mira, and she started speaking.
\fs24 \strokec2 \
\

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \strokec2 "How much do you know?", Veyr demanded.\'a0
\fs24 \strokec2 \

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \strokec2 "Wouldn't you like to know?" Mira replied
\fs24 \strokec2 \

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \strokec2 "Either way it's irrelevant. I don't deny the reports. I know you think you are doing the right thing but I assure you, you are not. The truth would kill more people than the lie." she said
\fs24 \strokec2 \
\

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \strokec2 "Think about it. 2 million people floating on the surface, no land, no food, no shelter, and no plan. No hope of escape. The open ocean would kill them, or they would die out." she continued
\fs24 \strokec2 \

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \strokec2 "And Sara? What did you monsters do to her?" said Mira, but to no response.
\fs24 \strokec2 \
\

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \strokec2 "Here's the deal. Go back and say nothing, and your family stays safe, if not, let's just say you want to take this deal."
\fs24 \strokec2 \

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \strokec2 Silence filled the room, but Mira wasn't listening. She couldn't stay silent, she had to save everyone.
\fs24 \strokec2 \

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \strokec2 "I will take your silence as a yes. Don't cross me. I am everywhere, and you are nothing to me." said Veyr.
\fs24 \strokec2 \

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \strokec2 Veyr left the room, and Mira was left alone. She couldn't think of Sara, or her family, so she instead chose to think about something much more important. Escape.
\fs24 \strokec2 \
\

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \strokec2 Eventually, Mira was taken to the surface as if nothing ever happened. The authorities pushed the narrative that Mira was kidnapped, and the guards saved her.
\fs24 \strokec2 \

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \strokec2 She slowly returned to her routine, but on the inside, she was broken. She knew she had to act like everything was normal, because she slowly noticed that the tank was monitored. Every painting of Veyr, every clock, even in the walls, were all secret cameras. One day, while walking with Ocean, she pulled her into a stairwell, away from the spying portraits.
\fs24 \strokec2 \

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \strokec2 "Hey! What are you doing?"
\fs24 \strokec2 \

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \strokec2 She told her everything, from the blackout, to the documents, to the kidnapping.
\fs24 \strokec2 \

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \strokec2 "Be quiet. We are going to run." said Mira
\fs24 \strokec2 \

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \strokec2 "What do you want us to do?"
\fs24 \strokec2 \
\

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \strokec2 Over the next few days, Mira and Ocean planned their escape. Mira remembers from the engine room that the monitoring logs go dark during blackouts, and Torin knows how to get to the emergency release latch from his time outside the tank. After a lot of deliberation, they decided that next blackout they would strike.
\fs24 \strokec2 \
\

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \strokec2 It only took three days, and the blackout came, Mira was selected to go to the engine room, and Ocean went with her, so she didn't get lost. Making sure no one saw them, they made their way to the hull inspection point. Luckily, Torin was able to slip her his keycard, and she almost got to the door, before they were caught.
\fs24 \strokec2 \
\

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \strokec2 "Stop right there!\'94 They realised they were surrounded, and the guards slowly approached. Ocean tried to run, but she was caught, and Mira was left alone. She decided she had to go alone.
\fs24 \strokec2 \
\

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \strokec2 She put the keycard into the slot, and closed the airlock. There was no going back. She watched Ocean get dragged away. She was still fighting, and still calling her name. She knew that she had come too far, and Ocean would never forgive her if she didn't take this chance. She opened the hatch, thinking of Sara, who never got the chance to make this choice. At least her disappearance would mean something, she thought.
\fs24 \strokec2 \
\

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \strokec2 She slowly floated up to the surface, expecting her lungs to burn, but the air was just cold, unlike the heavily filtered air in the tank. She was finally free, but at what cost? Her family, Ocean, Sara, still stuck below the glass.\'a0
\fs24 \strokec2 \
\

\fs29\fsmilli14667 \strokec2 Mira sat on a piece of ocean wreckage floating in the sea, looking down at the glass tank, with everyone inside. She wasn\'92t yet sure if what she did was selfish, or brave. Maybe it didn't matter anymore.
\fs24 \strokec2 \
\
\
\
}